# Replication package: Explainable machine-learning nowcasting in small samples

Code, data and results for the manuscript *Explainable machine-learning nowcasting in small samples
with an application to energy-related inflation in Türkiye* (energy-related CPI component of Türkiye,
monthly, 2012-2025).

## Contents

| Path | Content |
|---|---|
| `reproduce_all.py` | Single-command reproduction (smoke tests, feature construction, models, MIDAS benchmarks, evaluation, robustness, figures) |
| `REPRODUCIBILITY.md` | Pipeline description, determinism settings, design vocabulary |
| `requirements.txt` | Pinned Python environment (Python 3.12) |
| `config/features.yaml` | Single source of truth for feature sets, samples, information sets and model settings |
| `config/series_registry_template.csv` | EVDS series codes used by the download script |
| `src/common.py` | Shared data loading, learners, benchmarks, metrics, DM test, expanding-window driver |
| `src/data/` | Optional raw-data download and merge scripts (EVDS, EIA, CBRT, EPIAS); raw data are included, so these need not be run. The EVDS and EPIAS scripts read API credentials from a local `.env` file (`EVDS_API_KEY`, `EPIAS_USERNAME`, `EPIAS_PASSWORD`), which is not part of the package |
| `src/features/` | Monthly panel and intra-month MIDAS blocks from `data/raw/` |
| `src/models/` | Learners and combinations (origin-wise SHAP), MIDAS benchmarks, evaluation (DM-HLN, MCS), robustness |
| `src/figures/` | Main and supplementary figures; TIFF export for submission |
| `tests/test_smoke.py` | Smoke tests run at the start of `reproduce_all.py` |
| `data/raw/` | Raw inputs as downloaded (see Data sources) |
| `data/processed/` | `monthly_nowcasting_panel.csv` (168 month-end rows) and `midas_blocks.csv` |
| `results/` | All outputs of the canonical run reported in the manuscript (63 CSV files, environment report, summary) |
| `figures/` | Figures produced by the canonical run (PNG, 300 dpi) |

## Reproduction

```
conda create -n nowcast_repro python=3.12 -y
conda activate nowcast_repro
pip install -r requirements.txt
python reproduce_all.py
```

The run takes 60-90 minutes on a desktop CPU; the robustness step is the longest. The canonical run
reported in the manuscript was produced on Windows 11 with the package versions listed in
`results/environment_report.txt`. Elastic Net, LightGBM, the naive benchmark, the MIDAS regressions and
the VIF diagnostics reproduce bit-for-bit across Windows and Linux; XGBoost (`tree_method="exact"`,
single thread) is deterministic within a platform and differs across platforms by at most 0.025 RMSE
in the main tables.

## Naming conventions

- `ptf_*`: day-ahead market clearing price (PTF is the abbreviation used by EPIAS), in TL, USD or EUR per MWh.
- `*_w1` to `*_w4`: within-month block means (days 1-7, 8-14, 15-21, 22-end) used by the MIDAS benchmarks.
- `*_lagK`: K-month lag of a monthly variable.

## Design vocabulary

- `unconditional`: real-time nowcast; only predictors observable before the CPI release.
- `conditional`: headline-conditional nowcast; adds the contemporaneous headline inflation rate (`headline_mom`).
- `core` / `extended`: without / with the 12-month inflation-expectations block (156 / 144 monthly observations).

## Data sources

| Series | Source | File |
|---|---|---|
| CPI sub-group index "Housing, water, electricity, gas and other fuels" (TP.TUKFIY2025.04), headline CPI (TP.TUKFIY2025.GENEL), 12-month-ahead inflation expectations (TP.PKAUO.S01.E.U), USD/TRY selling rate (TP.DK.USD.S.YTL) | Central Bank of the Republic of Türkiye, Electronic Data Delivery System (EVDS), https://evds2.tcmb.gov.tr | `data/raw/evds/` |
| One-week repo (policy) rate | Central Bank of the Republic of Türkiye, policy-rate page | `data/raw/tcmb/` |
| Brent crude oil spot price, daily | U.S. Energy Information Administration, https://www.eia.gov/dnav/pet/hist/RBRTED.htm | `data/raw/eia/` |
| Day-ahead market clearing price (PTF), hourly, TL/USD/EUR | EPIAS Transparency Platform, https://seffaflik.epias.com.tr | `data/raw/epias/` |

The raw files are redistributed for reproducibility only; the data providers retain all rights, and any
reuse must cite the original source and comply with the providers' terms of use.

